Igor D. WonderLlama wrote:
> 
> The point is to make the game more dependant on skill, and less
> dependant on luck.  A game with an early wand of wishing is currently
> much easier to win than a game without.  I don't think anyone is
> denying that. 

If you would have said "In a game with an early wand of wishing you'll
get a bit farther than without one." I could have agreed.  My point of
view has been that a novice would gain a bit more insight(*) by getting
slightly deeper but would not *win*, because it requires much more than
wishes to survive in NH.  OTOH the experienced player won't necessarily
need a wand of wishing, because he can stay around much longer without
dying until he finds what he needs.  And for the medium skilled player
this single lucky game would, maybe, lead to his first ascension, maybe
not.  So what?

(*) This variance opens the opportunity to view a bit more of the
dungeons without really changing anything substancial.

> There are other combinations of items that you might
> find that make the game much easier than usual, I don't think anyone
> is denying that.  But the former is far more likely to occur, and has
> a much larger impact on the variance of the difficulty.

If you mean by "former" the wand of wishing, no.  An altar on level 1
had been created much more often in my games than wands of wishing.
And the altar is indeed of great importance to increase your chances
(b/u/c status, holy water, artifact weapon).

> When you set out to play a game of Nethack, it is a good thing to have
> an idea that the game will have a certain difficulty. 

We experience NH's "certain difficulty" and have got quite good feelings
about the variance.  There is some variance - and I like that in NH -
but the wand of wishing is quite irrelevant in the full NH context.

> It is a bad
> thing that some sessions will be very hard, while others will be very
> easy, entirely at random. 

The situation *is* that some NH games are overly hard while others are
easier, but surely *not* because of how the wand of wishing is defined.

I think levelling NH to a greater extent (start with wishing, continue
with altars, then the wand of death, ...ad infinitum) would devaluate
the game.  Keep variance, I'd say.

Janis

I am asking myself how many wands of wishing you usually find to make a
change of this specific item such an important topic.

